// @run
// @log 1.5
// @hint redundant type annotation: `r` is inferred as `f32[]` — remove it to lean on inference
// THE `@hint` IS NEW AND IS THE FIX'S OWN WITNESS: the linter can only call this annotation
// redundant because inference now reaches `f32[]` for `mk`'s return. Before, the binding was
// the only thing forcing the f32 list's wrapper into existence, which is what made this
// spelling the SILENT half of the pair.
// THE SIBLING OF `inferred-f32-list-return.vl`, and the one that was SILENT INVALID
// WASM rather than a reject: the same un-annotated `f32[]`-returning function, consumed
// through an ANNOTATED binding instead of a direct index.
//
// The annotation is what changes the outcome CLASS. `mk()[0]` (the sibling file) fails
// collection at the caller and rejects; `const r: f32[] = mk()` forces the f32 list's
// wrapper/backing types, the caller-side reject never fires, and the module is written —
// with `mk`'s functype result at the `"i32"` default over a body that returns the list's
// `(ref $array)`. `vl check` rc 0, `vl run`: "type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref
// $type)" inside `mk` itself.
//
// FIVE MORE SPELLINGS shared that fate, and they show the binding is INCIDENTAL — the
// callee is broken on its own:
//
//     const r: f32[] = mk()                 // this file
//     function main() { const r: f32[] = mk() … }   // a LOCAL binding
//     function main() { let r: f32[] = mk() … }     // a `let`
//     function use(a: f32[]) { … } use(mk())        // a call ARGUMENT
//     const r: f32[] = mk(); print(1)               // the binding is never READ
//     function mk() { … }  print(1)                 // `mk` is never CALLED AT ALL
//
// The last one is decisive: with no consumer of any kind, and only the `f32[]` annotation
// on `mk`'s own local, the module is still invalid. So this is not a call-site
// classification gap — the function's own result valtype disagrees with its own body, and
// every consumer merely inherits it.
//
// Held fixed against the sibling: one function, one local, one element, one leaf type.
// The only thing that moves is how the result is consumed.
//
// BOTH GRADUATED TOGETHER when `criClassify` learned to type a returned LOCAL from its own
// declaration (`letCellKind`) — the second of the two things the sibling's header named as
// prerequisites. The `f32[]`-return guard in `emitReturnValue` stays exactly where it is: it
// fires on the `"i32"` DEFAULT, so a correctly classified `"f32list"` return walks past it,
// and the spellings that still cannot be classified still draw the loud error.
function mk() {
  const xs: f32[] = [1.5]
  return xs
}
const r: f32[] = mk()
print(r[0])
